WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:13.120
Today we are going to have a little taste of how&nbsp;
smart an AI can be these days. And it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:19.920
these new AIs are not only smart enough to solve&nbsp;
some grade-school math problems, but get this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:27.840
a new development can perhaps even take a crack at&nbsp;
university-level problems. Is this even possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:34.000
or is this science fiction? Well, the&nbsp;
answer is yes, it is possible…kind of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.880
So, why kind of? Let me try to explain.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:48.000
This is OpenAI’s work from Oct 2021. The goal&nbsp;
is to have their AI understand these questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.760
understand the mathematics, and reason about a&nbsp;
possible solution for grade-school problems. Hmm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:03.280
alright. So, this means that the GPT-3 AI might&nbsp;
be suitable for the substrate of the solution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:12.240
What is that? GPT-3 is a technique that can&nbsp;
understand text, try to finish your sentences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:18.800
even build websites, and more. So, can&nbsp;
it even deal with these test questions?

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.360
Let’s see together. Hold on to your&nbsp;
papers, because in goes a grade-school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.960
level question. A little math brain&nbsp;
teaser if you will. And out comes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:36.960
my goodness. Is that right? Here, out comes&nbsp;
not only the correct solution to the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:43.360
but even the thought process that led to this&nbsp;
solution. Imagine someone claiming that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:50.560
had developed an AI this capable ten years ago.&nbsp;
This person would have been locked into an asylum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:57.360
And now, it is all there, right in&nbsp;
front of our eyes. Absolutely amazing.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:03.600
Okay, but, how amazing? Well, it can’t&nbsp;
get everything right all the time. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.280
even close. If we do everything right,&nbsp;
we can expect it to be correct about 35%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.920
of the time. Not perfect, not even&nbsp;
close, but it is an amazing step forward.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:24.160
So what is the key here? Well, yes, you guessed&nbsp;
it right. The usual suspects. A big neural network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:31.200
and lots of training data, the key&nbsp;
numbers are 175 billion model parameters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.440
and it needs to read a few thousand&nbsp;
problems and their solutions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:41.760
as training samples. That is a big rocket,&nbsp;
and lots of rocket fuel if you will.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:49.360
But, this is nothing compared to what is to come.&nbsp;
Now, believe it or not, here is a followup paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:56.000
from just a few months later, January 2022&nbsp;
that claims to do something even better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:03.840
This is not from OpenAI, but it piggybacks on&nbsp;
OpenAI technology as you will see in a moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.680
And this work promises that it can solve&nbsp;
university-level problems. And when I saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:16.400
this reading the paper, I thought…really? Now,&nbsp;
grade school materials, okay, that is a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.360
leap forward, but solving university-level&nbsp;
math exams? Now we’re talking! That’s where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:27.360
the gloves come off. I am really curious to see&nbsp;
what this can do! Let’s have a look together.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.200
Some of these brain teasers smell very&nbsp;
much like MIT to me. Surprisingly short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:41.280
and elegant questions, that often seem much&nbsp;
easier than they are. However, all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:48.240
require a solid understanding of fundamentals,&nbsp;
and sometimes even a hint of creativity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:57.600
Let’s see. Yes! That is indeed right. These are&nbsp;
MIT introductory course questions. I love it. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:04.560
can it answer them? Now, if you have been holding&nbsp;
on to your papers, now, squeeze that paper, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:13.840
let’s see the results together…my goodness. These&nbsp;
are all correct. Flying colors! Perfect accuracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:21.200
at least on these questions. This is swift&nbsp;
progress in just a few months. Absolutely amazing.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.480
So, how is this black magic done? Yes,&nbsp;
I know that’s what you’re waiting for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.560
let’s pop the hood, and look inside together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.960
Um-hm. Alright! Two key differences&nbsp;
from OpenAI’s GPT3-based solution.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:44.560
Difference number one. It gets additional&nbsp;
guidance. For instance, it is told what topic are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.720
we talking about, what code library to reach out&nbsp;
for, and what is the definition of mathematical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:57.520
concepts, for instance, what is a singular value&nbsp;
decomposition. I would argue that this is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:04.160
too bad, students typically get taught these&nbsp;
things before the exam too. In my opinion, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:11.040
key is that this additional guidance is done in an&nbsp;
automated manner. The more automated, the better.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.080
Difference number two. The&nbsp;
substrate here is not GPT-3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:22.640
at least, not directly, but Codex. What is&nbsp;
that? Codex is OpenAI’s GPT language model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:29.840
that was fine-tuned to be excellent at one&nbsp;
thing. And that is, writing computer programs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:37.360
or, finishing your code. And as we’ve seen in&nbsp;
a previous episode, it really is excellent. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:44.160
instance, it can not only be asked to explain a&nbsp;
piece of code, even if it is written in assembly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:51.200
Or, create a pong game in 30 seconds. But,&nbsp;
we can also give it plain text descriptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:58.080
about a space game, and it will write it.&nbsp;
Codex is super powerful. And now, it can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:05.280
used to solve previously unseen university-level&nbsp;
math problems. Now that is really something.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:11.360
And, it can even generate a bunch of&nbsp;
new questions, and these are bona fide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:17.040
real questions. Not just exchanging the&nbsp;
numbers, the new questions often require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:22.720
completely different insights to solve&nbsp;
these problems. A little creativity I see!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:31.600
Well done little AI! So, how good are these? Well,&nbsp;
according to human evaluators, they are almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:39.040
as good as the ones written by other humans. And&nbsp;
thus, these can even be used to provide more and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:46.160
more training data for such an AI. More fuel for&nbsp;
that rocket. And, good kind of fuel. Excellent.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:53.520
And, it doesn’t end there, in the meantime,&nbsp;
as of February 2022, scientists at OpenAI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:07:00.480
are already working on a followup paper that&nbsp;
solves no less than high-school mathematical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:07.520
olympiad problems. These problems require a solid&nbsp;
understanding of fundamentals, proper reasoning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:14.960
and often even that is not enough. Many of these&nbsp;
tasks put up a seemingly impenetrable wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:21.200
and climbing the wall typically requires a real&nbsp;
creative spark. Yes, this means that these can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:28.560
get quite tough. And their new method is doing&nbsp;
really well at these. Once again, not perfect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:36.240
not even close, but it can solve about 30 to 40%&nbsp;
of these tasks, a that is a remarkable hit rate.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:43.120
Now we see that all of these works are&nbsp;
amazing, and they have their own tradeoffs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:49.520
They are good and bad at different things and&nbsp;
have different requirements. And most of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:55.680
they all have their own limitations. Thus, none&nbsp;
of these works should be thought of as an AI that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:01.280
just automatically does human-level math. However,&nbsp;
what we now see is that there is swift progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:08.720
in this area, and amazing new papers are popping&nbsp;
up not every year, pretty much every month.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:15.440
And, this is an excellent place to apply The&nbsp;
First Law Of Papers, which says that research is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:21.520
a process. Do not look at where we are, look at&nbsp;
where we will be two more papers down the line.

